It would be nice if we didn't have to do these recursive calls.
I think the code (and datastructures) could be simplified.
Currently, we have:
```
type bufferEventChunkQueue struct {
	head, tail *bufferEventChunk
}
type bufferEventChunk struct {
	events     [bufferEventChunkArrSize]Event
	head, tail int32
	next       *bufferEventChunk // linked-list element
}
```

It's a bit sad that each chunk has to carry head/tail counters.
I think those counters can be lifted to bufferEventQueue
(drop the head/tail from bufferEventChunk):
```
type bufferEventChunkQueue struct {
  head, tail *bufferEventChunk
  headChunkIdx, tailChunkIdx int      
}
```

Enqueue becomes pretty easy:
```
func (l *bufferEventChunkQueue) enqueue(e Event) {
   if l.tail == nil || l.tailChunkIdx == bufferEventChunkArrSize {
      // Add new chunk; this either creates or updates l.tail
   }
   l.tail.events[l.tailChunkIdx] = e
   l.tailChunkIdx++
}
```

Dequeue is similarly easy
```
func (l *bufferEventChunkQueue) dequeue() (e Event, ok bool) {
   // You always dequeue from head
   e = l.head.events[l.headChunkIdx]
  l.headChunkIdx++
  if l.headChunkIdx == bufferEventChunkArrSize {
     // free l.head
  }
}
```

